# Mathematical algorithm candidates and tile contracts · `spec:nrz:algorithms-concept`

**Caveat (Concept standing)** · `cav:nrz:algorithms-standing`

This concept explores several algorithms for one mathematical capability and algorithms contracted over tiles, building on (`dec:nrz:matrix-loans-kernel-family`), (`dec:nrz:transcendental-families`), (`spec:nrz:lc-grid-tiles`) and (`spec:nrz:reuse-concept`). Its general candidate proposals extend the independently audited candidate principle already adopted for certified transcendental kernels in (`prin:nrz:transcendental-waveform-algorithm-candidates`), preserving that principle's simplest-first admission and coexistence policy. Every new obligation below has a `proposal:` label and remains nonbinding pending a design decision; definitions and examples describe the proposed model, not delivered support. No existing member, tariff, layout, target or public extension mechanism changes.

**Definition (Capability and candidate)** · `def:nrz:algorithms-candidate`

A capability names an original mathematical operation and its observable assurance, including domain, source correspondence, output meanings and permitted outcomes. A candidate is a particular finite algorithm and arithmetic schedule that establishes that assurance on a declared admitted subdomain. Distinct schedules can return different enclosures within the same requested tracked-error contract under (`prin:nrz:descriptor-binding-reproducibility`); certified outcomes for the same exact question agree under (`thm:nrz:decision-reproducibility`), with tile-size variation an analogue in (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-size`). A matrix product and an elementwise product are different capabilities; two multiplication schedules for the same contraction can be candidates. The closed member family of (`rule:nrz:matrix-loans-kernel-family-contract`) identifies operations and authentication boundaries, not permission to substitute arbitrary code.

**Proposal (Candidate contract)** · `proposal:nrz:algorithms-contract`

Propose that each candidate declare capability and candidate revision, exact original operation and correspondence, mathematical and admitted domains, formats and environment, input/source/companion coverage, outputs and joint dependencies, requested target interpretation, complete error law and checked arithmetic schedule. Distinguish total uncertainty, numerical error, endpoint slack and exact special cases; identify all guards, typed refusals and unresolved outcomes. State preparation, application, refinement, checking and publication resources, storage/retention lifetimes, finite limits and independent evidence identities. The member profile of (`rule:nrz:matrix-loans-kernel-family-contract`), operation families of (`dec:nrz:transcendental-families`) and numerical dependency closure of (`def:nrz:reuse-numerical-artifact`) supply the existing boundaries; a shared capability name supplies none of this evidence by itself.

**Proposal (Admission, coexistence and retirement)** · `proposal:nrz:algorithms-lifecycle`

Propose independent admission of every candidate against the capability contract, with a checked proof over its entire admitted domain, numerical controls and measured resource figures under (`proposal:nrz:algorithms-evidence`). Each capability revision designates and records a reference candidate, initially the simplest candidate to implement and audit, extending (`prin:nrz:transcendental-waveform-algorithm-candidates`) to general mathematical capabilities. Its eligibility satisfies every filter in (`proposal:nrz:algorithms-selection`) except a caller preference; reference status grants no exemption from domain, target, tile, environment, resource or reproducibility premises. Faster candidates add choices and never displace the reference. A changed schedule, proof, arithmetic environment or relevant dependency creates a new immutable revision and re-admission scope; changing capability meaning creates a separate capability revision. Retirement prevents new selections while preserving interpretable historical records and owned evidence under (`def:nrz:reuse-provenance`); invalidated evidence is explicitly marked and cannot authorize fresh reuse. The reference may retire only on invalidated or withdrawn evidence or a changed capability; retirement requires designating and recording an independently admitted successor reference or retiring the capability revision. Historical reproducibility does not require executing a withdrawn unsafe candidate. This proposes no plugin registry or relaxation of (`rule:nrz:matrix-loans-kernel-family-contract`).

**Proposal (Selection and accountable fallback)** · `proposal:nrz:algorithms-selection`

Propose that the existing plan/decision owner of (`req:nrz:tiles-streams-plan-owner`) choose the candidate and tile size/layout jointly for a request or tile. Preserve the allocation in (`dec:nrz:transcendental-families`): CAS retains exact mathematical authority, bindings retains original-operation correspondence and reading/companion inventories, scalar owns enclosure primitives, and matrix owns numerical region kernels and application evidence, including actual native guards. Filter by original domain and sources, target, tile shape/spacing/mask/readiness, format/environment, resource ceilings and declared reproducibility policy before estimating cost. Caller preference is limited here to the existing optional tile-preference hint of (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-size`), not a new public algorithm selector; it cannot exclude an otherwise eligible reference candidate under (`proposal:nrz:algorithms-lifecycle`). A recorded replay fixes the recorded candidate under (`proposal:nrz:algorithms-record`) instead of selecting the current reference or a faster candidate. Rejection, refinement and fallback retain typed reasons and spent work; a resource refusal is not mathematical impossibility. Joint choice uses the one plan/decision owner and adds no scheduler under (`dec:nrz:lc-grid-execution-flow`); it does not erase the decision barriers of (`def:nrz:lc-grid-barrier`).

**Proposal (Choice record and replay)** · `proposal:nrz:algorithms-record`

Propose retaining the capability/candidate/proof revisions, the capability revision's designated reference and its designation revision, complete numerical dependency key, requested target, input versions, tile map and original index order, masks and arrival snapshot, arithmetic environment, selection policy and tie break, reduction/anchor choices, resource allowances and actual fallback/refinement path in the result's owned provenance. A recorded replay fixes the recorded candidate revisions and path, even if the reference designation or selection policy has changed; unavailable or invalidated authority yields an explicit replay refusal rather than silent reselection. A fresh selection can produce a separately recorded result. A deterministic policy fixes choices for identical recorded inputs; bitwise replay additionally fixes instruction schedule, reduction tree and permitted environment. Reproducibility within tracked error instead permits differing enclosures under (`prin:nrz:descriptor-binding-reproducibility`) while preserving certified facts under (`thm:nrz:decision-reproducibility`), as tile-size variation does in (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-size`). Cache matching includes every dependency of the promised claim under (`def:nrz:reuse-numerical-artifact`); equal output bytes or a shared candidate name cannot authenticate changed inputs.

**Definition (Collection capability)** · `def:nrz:algorithms-collection`

A collection capability has an original indexed set or ordered sequence as a mathematical operand: sum, scan, convolution and matrix contraction are examples. Its order, boundary convention and output incidence belong to the question, not to an execution tile. The collection may span tiles with a proved carry, partial reduction or boundary exchange preserving that question; a tile is only an execution partition. These are not tile-only substitutes for independent per-cell answers (`def:nrz:algorithms-candidate`), (`proposal:nrz:algorithms-tile-substitution`).

**Definition (Tile-only contract)** · `def:nrz:algorithms-tile-only`

A tile-only candidate for a per-cell capability establishes the same original answer at each selected cell, but its cross-cell recurrence or representation has correctness, error or resource premises over an execution tile: shape, coordinate map, spacing, mask and arrival state. Rotation recurrence and a shared exponent are examples. A singleton may be a degenerate valid case without providing the algorithm's advantage. The execution tile is not a new mathematical operand or decision barrier (`spec:nrz:lc-grid-tiles`), (`def:nrz:lc-grid-barrier`); collection capabilities, shared preparation and batched independent laws have separate definitions here.

**Definition (Tile-level shared preparation)** · `def:nrz:algorithms-shared-preparation`

Tile-level shared preparation computes reusable constants, range-reduction data, coefficients or a uniform expansion for compatible cells, while the resulting per-cell law remains independently applicable. Shared range reduction and a shared Chebyshev expansion are examples; using one preparation does not assert equal arguments or independent sources. The preparation's dependency closure, admitted domain, amortized cost and lifetime follow (`def:nrz:reuse-numerical-artifact`), with the candidate contract of (`proposal:nrz:algorithms-contract`).

**Definition (Batched independent laws)** · `def:nrz:algorithms-batched`

Batched independent laws apply an independently justified cell schedule to each selected cell, possibly using SIMD or a batched traversal. Grouping affects execution cost and layout without introducing recurrence state, shared-representation error or a collection-valued mathematical operand. Shared coefficient preparation is classified separately under (`def:nrz:algorithms-shared-preparation`); combining mechanisms retains the evidence for each class rather than turning vectorization alone into a tile-only algorithm (`proposal:nrz:algorithms-contract`).

**Proposal (Tile contract extension)** · `proposal:nrz:algorithms-tile-contract`

Propose adding to (`proposal:nrz:algorithms-contract`) exact cell/output incidence, allowed shape and layout, equal-spacing or shared-exponent premises where used, source correlations, ordering, valid/absent/refused masks, required arrival prefix or complete-set condition, and scratch/state ownership. These are class-specific execution obligations: a tile-only per-cell candidate proves its cross-cell law, a collection candidate proves partition/carry correspondence, shared preparation proves its reuse scope, and batched independent laws retain their cell proofs. Give a uniform per-cell bound indexed by position and tile size where there are per-cell outputs, and the appropriate joint or aggregate error bound; include initialization, shared constants, accumulation and restoration. State which masked cells are skipped, traversed internally or terminate a recurrence, and distinguish a missing observation from zero. Define partial and out-of-order arrival, cancellation, flush, splitting, changed inputs and refinement, including when to discard state or re-anchor. These proposed additions specialize the cost and identity boundaries of (`def:nrz:lc-grid-cost`) and (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-size`).

**Proposal (Substitution and per-tile charges)** · `proposal:nrz:algorithms-tile-substitution`

Propose replacing independent evaluation only after the tile premises and output incidence prove the same original requested answers, coverage, companions and targets. An aggregate sum or transform cannot replace a vector of per-cell function answers. If spacing, masks, arrival, targets or storage violate a premise, split or re-anchor with proved coverage, select an admitted independent candidate, or return the existing unresolved/refusal outcome; never fabricate missing cells. Count preparation/reuse checks, mask scans, sorting, shared constants, internal recurrence steps, rejected attempts, anchors, refinement, scratch and publication once at their actual tile/owner boundary under (`def:nrz:lc-grid-cost`). Amortize against actual evaluated cells, reporting hidden traversed positions separately; no per-cell exact proof work is introduced into the numerical fast path.

**Example (Rotation recurrence and bounded segments)** · `ex:nrz:algorithms-rotation`

For original arguments x_j=x_0+j*h with proved common spacing, a possible tile-only per-cell candidate propagates a numerical point (midpoint) pair q_j=(c_j,s_j) with one scalar Euclidean radius E_j for the enclosed claim. A separately enclosed rotation supplies numerical coefficients a≈cos(h), b≈sin(h); the point update is c_(j+1)=a*c_j-b*s_j, s_(j+1)=b*c_j+a*s_j, using the old pair for both components. For a fixed original pair, its actual error e_j in the Euclidean vector norm satisfies e_(j+1)<=(1+eta)*e_j+eta+delta_j, where eta bounds the approximate rotation's error in the induced operator norm, delta_j bounds execution error and the exact point pair has norm one. From certified e_0<=E_0 follows e_j<=(1+eta)^j*E_0+sum_(k=0)^(j-1)(1+eta)^(j-1-k)*(eta+delta_k). The candidate computes E_j by outward evaluation of this majorant or its recurrence, including the radius calculation's rounding; near-linear small-error growth is not a size-independent guarantee. The candidate publishes component enclosures [c_j-E_j,c_j+E_j] and [s_j-E_j,s_j+E_j], with checked outward endpoint export and its rounding. Whole-support publication requires E_0 and eta to bound every feasible original pair and rotation uniformly, retaining shared uncertain h and its position-dependent phase support; a separate nominal-input error claim is still owed under (`proposal:nrz:algorithms-tile-contract`). These are projections of a common enclosed pair, not a feasible rectangle of independent components.

**Example (Componentwise interval wrapping)** · `ex:nrz:algorithms-rotation-wrapping`

Propagating componentwise intervals instead of the point pair and scalar radius of (`ex:nrz:algorithms-rotation`) is a different algorithm and needs its own bound. Even with exact rotation coefficients and no added roundoff, equal component radii are multiplied each step by |cos(h)|+|sin(h)|, strictly greater than one away from axis rotations and equal to one at axis rotations. For small positive equally spaced h=2*pi/n, the factor over a period tends to exp(2*pi) as n grows. Repeated rectangular relaxation loses the pair's correlation; the scalar Euclidean-radius recurrence cannot be claimed for that wrapping implementation (`proposal:nrz:algorithms-evidence`).

**Proposal (Recurrence anchors)** · `proposal:nrz:algorithms-anchors`

For (`ex:nrz:algorithms-rotation`), propose choosing a finite segment length from the full position-dependent error bound and requested target, re-anchoring by an independently admitted direct enclosure at the original argument before that bound exceeds its allocation. The segment length limits anchor spacing within a tile, not the tile size; one tile may contain several certified segments. Renormalizing rounded pairs alone does not bound phase drift. Sparse masks either traverse every intervening step and charge its error/work, or start a fresh certified anchor; compacting selected indices cannot silently replace h by another spacing. A separately enclosed stride rotation R(m*h) is another possible candidate, with the exact index gap m, original phase correspondence and its own reduction/rounding bound recorded and charged; it does not fabricate intervening observations. Out-of-order arrivals wait in bounded storage or use independent anchors with recorded indices. Refinement invalidates affected recurrence state and recomputes from an authenticated anchor; an anchor reset removes accumulated execution error only to the extent proved, never original input uncertainty (`proposal:nrz:algorithms-tile-contract`).

**Table (Algorithm classes and their evidence)** · `tab:nrz:algorithms-examples`

|Example|Class|Judgment and additional evidence|
|-------|-----|--------------------------------|
|Rotation recurrence|Tile-only per-cell candidate|Ordering, common spacing, point-pair/radius representation, position-dependent drift and anchors are essential (`ex:nrz:algorithms-rotation`), (`proposal:nrz:algorithms-anchors`).|
|Shared range reduction or prepared constant|Tile-level shared preparation|One admitted stratum or dependency closure serves several cells; independent per-cell reduction still exists. Shared constants need import-error and domain evidence (`def:nrz:algorithms-shared-preparation`).|
|Batched/vectorized polynomial evaluation|Batched independent laws|A fixed independently justified cell schedule can execute in SIMD lanes. Shared coefficients or degree selection additionally need shared-preparation evidence; vectorization alone introduces no cross-cell error law (`def:nrz:algorithms-batched`).|
|Chebyshev/Clenshaw evaluation over a shared range|Tile-level shared preparation|Clenshaw evaluates one series at one argument and is not inherently tile-only. Sharing an expansion over a tile range needs checked range mapping and a uniform residual (`def:nrz:algorithms-shared-preparation`).|
|Shared mid-radius or exponent|Tile-only per-cell candidate|A shared representation needs range and restoration laws for every covered cell; outliers need another grouping or fallback. Sharing an exponent proves no source independence (`def:nrz:algorithms-tile-only`).|
|Compensated or pairwise sum|Collection capability|The original indexed reduction needs finite rounding, compensation, tree, overflow and mask laws. Partial reductions across tiles retain a proved combination law; independent cell answers are not substitutes (`def:nrz:algorithms-collection`).|
|Prefix computations|Collection capability|An ordered scan retains every prefix's error and authenticated incoming state; split tiles need a proved carry boundary. Arrival reordering is not a free mathematical permutation (`def:nrz:algorithms-collection`).|
|Transform-based convolution|Collection capability|The original collection, boundary convention, padding/aliasing, transform normalization and propagated error determine the requested convolution; cross-tile boundary exchange needs proof (`def:nrz:algorithms-collection`).|
|Transform-based multipoint evaluation|Tile-only per-cell candidate|Outputs arise from the joint transform, so their errors depend on cross-cell work; grid premises, reconstruction and errors need proof under (`def:nrz:algorithms-tile-only`). Reusable twiddle or subproduct data is its shared preparation under (`def:nrz:algorithms-shared-preparation`), not an independently applicable output law. An FFT is not automatically an algorithm for arbitrary irregular samples.|
|Blocked matrix multiplication|Collection capability|The original contraction spans its inner index set; panel grouping, edge masks and cross-tile partial sums retain a proved accumulation/carry law. Matrix shape alone certifies no changed summation schedule (`def:nrz:algorithms-collection`).|

**Proposal (Sizing, layout and stream readiness)** · `proposal:nrz:algorithms-streams`

Propose that the one plan/decision owner jointly choose candidate, tile size and layout under (`proposal:nrz:algorithms-selection`), (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-size`) and (`req:nrz:tiles-streams-plan-owner`), without an added scheduler under (`dec:nrz:lc-grid-execution-flow`). Range, spacing, scratch, output extent and error laws constrain compatible layouts; a proved recurrence segment length limits anchor spacing, not the size of a tile containing several segments (`proposal:nrz:algorithms-anchors`). Compare total cost including anchors and fill latency; shortening or regrouping preserves original cell identities, branch coverage and target. On streams, preparation may use stable declared dependencies, but unarrived values cannot discharge actual-input guards. Execute a prefix only with a prefix law; a collection-wide transform waits for its required operand set, possibly spanning tiles, while a tile-only transform waits for its required filled set or a declared final partial-tile rule. Bound retained state and buffers, preserve older readers and companions, and choose fallback or typed unresolved outcomes on flush when premises remain absent. Host-controlled arrival and flush remain those of (`dec:nrz:lc-grid-execution-flow`); arrival order does not redefine the question or authorize unbounded waiting storage.

**Proposal (Independent candidate evidence)** · `proposal:nrz:algorithms-evidence`

Propose a mathematical proof for original-function correspondence, complete domain and guard coverage, analytic remainder and finite arithmetic error for every candidate revision, plus an independent numerical evaluator or exact rational reconstruction with separately justified bounds. Per-cell controls cover special values, extrema, zeros, boundaries, tiny/large values, subnormal/overflow behavior, target/refusal distinctions and corrupted evidence. Compare faster and simple candidates to that independent authority and to their own promised bounds; pairwise agreement, overlapping enclosures or trigonometric identities alone prove neither correct. Differences within tracked error are legitimate; disjoint certified claims trigger investigation of both candidates and the oracle. This extends the independent finite-schedule discipline of (`req:nrz:transcendental-schedule-proof`) without adopting a new trusted implementation.

**Proposal (Tile evidence and measured advantage)** · `proposal:nrz:algorithms-tile-evidence`

Propose testing entire tile output maps and joint/aggregate claims independently, including size-one and maximal admitted tiles, every recurrence position, anchor boundaries, long runs, sparse/empty masks, missing and out-of-order arrivals, source mutation, splitting/refinement, cancellation and partial/full flush. Compare equivalent original questions under several tilings and permutations only where permutation is mathematically allowed. Use deliberately broken spacing, omitted steps, wrong masks, changed constants and stale carry state to check refusal and containment. Prove a bound for all admitted sizes rather than inferring it from sampled runs. Measure cold/warm preparation, actual visits and fallbacks, numerical time, proof/check work and resident/retained memory under (`def:nrz:lc-grid-cost`); a speed claim states workload and uncertainty and never substitutes for proof (`proposal:nrz:algorithms-evidence`).

**Example (Initial capability instances)** · `ex:nrz:algorithms-first-candidates`

Concept instances of the candidate model are explicit Taylor sine/cosine with guarded Cody–Waite reduction first, only on its proved range, with separately certified reduction or refusal outside it under (`prin:nrz:transcendental-waveform-algorithm-candidates`) and the full error accounting and quadrant coverage of (`req:nrz:transcendental-waveform-reduction`); the guarded numerical phase enclosure of (`req:nrz:cas-trig-design-phase-enclosure`), retaining origin exclusion, axes and wrapped alternatives under (`req:nrz:cas-trig-design-two-argument-phase`); the existing positive square-root member (the nonnegative branch, including zero) under (`rule:nrz:matrix-loans-square-root-member`), distinct from the stricter prepared power domain of (`dec:nrz:matrix-loans-dyadic-power-member`); and retained prepared expm1 under (`dec:nrz:matrix-loans-prepared-expm1-slice`), including the cancellation-sensitive alternating Horner schedule of (`req:nrz:transcendental-small-schedule`). Each numerical phase chart candidate still earns its own inverse-relation or arctangent enclosure proof and resource admission; symbolic chart authority alone is insufficient. The first tile-only experiment worth evaluating is the equally spaced waveform rotation of (`ex:nrz:algorithms-rotation`), checked against direct Taylor enclosures and independent bounds with explicit anchors. These are concept instances of existing contracts and a proposed experiment, not new adopted requirements, executable-delivery claims or universal performance rankings.

